course: dictionary
lesson: 13034
title: Falsifying: making the untrue look true
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Falsifying comes from false, which means not true. When you meet a new word, check the words around it. «The clerk was caught falsifying the test scores.» Caught shows that someone did something wrong, so falsifying must mean changing the scores to make them untrue.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. Swap in «changing» and see if it still makes sense: «The clerk was caught changing the test scores.» It still works, and now the wrong part is clear: the scores were changed so they would not be true.
explain: Science uses the word in a different but linked way. «A single black swan falsifies the idea that all swans are white.» One black swan shows the idea is wrong, so falsifies means proves to be wrong.
fact: Falsifying means changing a document, record or result so that it is untrue and tricks people.
fact: «Falsifying documents» and «falsifying evidence» are cheating and against the law.
fact: In science, falsifying means showing that an idea or theory is wrong.
fact: The verb forms are falsify, falsifies, falsified and falsifying.
fact: The word comes from «false», meaning not true.
exercise: «The accountant was sent to prison for falsifying the company's records.» What does falsifying mean here? | Checking that records are tidy and neat | Copying records onto a new page | *Changing records so they are untrue, to trick people | Keeping records safe in a locked cupboard
exercise: «A single black swan falsifies the idea that all swans are white.» What does falsifies mean in this science sentence? | Forgets the idea completely | Explains the idea in simple words | *Proves the idea is wrong | Makes the idea stronger and more popular
exercise: Which word is a verb form of falsify? | falseness | falsity | *falsified | falsehood
test: «The boy was punished for falsifying evidence to get out of trouble.» What does falsifying evidence mean? | Losing evidence on the way home | Sharing evidence with a friend | Finding evidence in a dark room | *Changing evidence so it is untrue, to trick people
test: «The scientists tested the theory, and their results falsified it.» What does falsified mean here? | *Proved to be wrong | Checked and approved by everyone | Written down in a neat book | Made very popular
exam: Which sentence uses falsifying to mean cheating? | The bird was falsifying its song in the tall oak tree. | The farmer was falsifying the clouds over the field. | We enjoyed falsifying a sunny picture of the park for fun. | *The judge said the man was guilty of falsifying the bank documents.
